.TH NSDIFF 1 "September 2026" "nsdiff @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nsdiff \- compare Linux process execution environments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nsdiff
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " PID_A PID_B"
.br
.B nsdiff
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " --against FILE PID"
.br
.B nsdiff
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " --snapshots FILE_A FILE_B"
.br
.B nsdiff
.RI "--capture FILE PID [--snapshot-format native|portable]"
.br
.B nsdiff
.RI "--convert-snapshot IN OUT --snapshot-format native|portable"
.br
.B nsdiff
.RI "[--json] --snapshot-info|--verify-snapshot|--snapshot-compat|--snapshot-id|--semantic-id|--snapshot-manifest FILE"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Compare namespaces, effective credentials, resource limits, selected cgroup v2
files, network sets, selected mounts, exec-time environment and security state.
This is a bounded diagnostic model, not an exhaustive process dump. No process
state is changed. Root is not required; restricted fields become unavailable.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Comparison JSON schema 1. Incompatible with section filtering and other modes.
.TP
.B --only-differences
Show only semantic differences.
.TP
.B --explain
Show differences and explain their possible effects.
.TP
.B --summary
Show per-section comparable, different and unavailable counts.
.TP
.BR -q ", " --quiet
Write no normal comparison output. Use the exit status.
.TP
.B --section NAME
Repeat to select namespaces, credentials, limits, cgroup, network, mounts,
environment or security. The name all selects all sections. Without an explicit
output mode, section selection uses compact difference output.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display complete option help.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Display the build version.
.SH SNAPSHOT OPTIONS
.TP
.B --capture FILE PID
Capture a process. Default native v3 is ABI/endian-dependent.
.TP
.B --snapshot-format native|portable
Select capture/conversion output. Portable schema v1 is architecture-independent
JSON with exact integer and byte encodings, imported by the ordinary diff engine.
.TP
.B --convert-snapshot IN OUT
Convert between native v1/v2/v3 and portable v1. Requires --snapshot-format.
.TP
.B --against FILE PID
Compare a snapshot with a live process.
.TP
.B --snapshots A B
Compare two saved snapshots; formats may differ.
.TP
.B --snapshot-info FILE
Display metadata. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --verify-snapshot FILE
Validate the container and semantic model. Supports --json. Native integrity hashes
are checked; portable v1 has no stored checksum.
.TP
.B --snapshot-compat FILE
Display format/layout compatibility. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --snapshot-id FILE
Native raw payload SHA-256 identity (nsdiff:sha256:). Portable files return semantic
identity with the distinct nsdiff:semantic-sha256: prefix. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --semantic-id FILE
Canonical architecture-independent model identity for either format. Excludes
producer, provenance, PID/starttime and native padding. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --snapshot-manifest FILE
Display identity, integrity and provenance. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --snapshot-schema
Display supported native/portable schemas. Supports --json.
.TP
.B --export-snapshot-json FILE
Redacted comparison-schema diagnostic export. This is not an importable snapshot.
.SH STREAMS
FILE may be - for stdin on reads or stdout on capture/conversion. Two comparison
inputs cannot both use stdin. Capture status goes to stderr when writing stdout.
File arguments following options accept leading dashes. Use -- to end option
parsing before positional PIDs. Quote filenames containing spaces or shell syntax.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful operation or no comparable differences. Unavailable fields can exist.
.TP
.B 1
Successful comparison with semantic differences.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid invocation, malformed snapshot, fatal collection or output failure.
.SH SECURITY
Lossless snapshots contain private environment values. File writes use 0600,
randomized adjacent temporary files, fsync and atomic rename followed by directory
fsync. Existing destination symlinks are replaced without writing to their targets.
Shell redirections control their own permissions. Proxy values are redacted only
in diagnostic output. Review other diagnostic values before sharing.
.PP
SHA-256 is not a signature. No authenticity is claimed. Files have an 8 MiB input
limit and strict field bounds. Fatal signals can leave a private temporary file.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Collection is not atomic across fields. Starttime/pidfd checks reduce process
lifetime races but cannot freeze process configuration. A matching procfs/PID
namespace is required. Native files require the same ABI and byte order.
.PP
Network sets are bounded to 64 entries each; watched environment values to 4095
bytes. Truncated fields are unavailable. Only documented watched fields are
compared. Cgroup lookup assumes a conventional target /sys/fs/cgroup mount.
Legacy JSON schema 1 strings use byte-codepoint escaping; portable byte objects
explicitly distinguish UTF-8 and hex. See the format documentation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
nsdiff --explain $$ 1234
nsdiff --capture before.json $$ --snapshot-format portable
nsdiff --against before.json $$ --summary
nsdiff --convert-snapshot before.json local.snap --snapshot-format native
nsdiff --capture - $$ | nsdiff --verify-snapshot -
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc (5),
.BR namespaces (7),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR cgroups (7)
